	array(
		'question_text'  => 'An LMFT wants input from a licensed health care professional who specializes in the client’s condition. Which approach is most accurate?',
		'option_a'       => 'A signed authorization is always required before any treatment consultation',
		'option_b'       => 'Because both people are licensed, the LMFT may disclose the full record for any purpose',
		'option_c'       => 'The LMFT should confirm the treatment purpose and legal basis, use relevant information, and de-identify or limit details when feasible',
		'option_d'       => 'The LMFT should post the facts in a closed professional group because membership makes the discussion confidential',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. California CMIA and HIPAA, when applicable, permit some provider-to-provider disclosures for treatment without a separate authorization; the analysis is contextual.
		B. Incorrect. Licensure does not authorize unlimited disclosure or a non-treatment purpose. The information and recipient must fit a lawful basis.
