		'option_c'       => 'Quietly add the information to the record without informing the supervisor',
		'option_d'       => 'Ask the client not to discuss the attempt until the supervisor is available',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Correct. Client safety and accurate supervisory disclosure cannot wait. The registered AMFT should address current risk and use the established supervisor or alternative on-call procedure promptly.
		B. Incorrect. Scheduled supervision does not override urgent client-safety needs. Delay could worsen risk and violates the purpose of emergency availability procedures.
		C. Incorrect. A lawful correction to documentation may be needed, but a silent edit does not ensure current risk is assessed or that the supervisor can exercise oversight.
		D. Incorrect. Recruiting the client into concealment is unethical, distorts care, and increases risk.
		CTA Exam Strategy: When the facts include possible immediate danger, choose safety action and prompt qualified supervisory contact before routine scheduling concerns.
		Remediation: Return to Workbook 3, Chapter 5: Clinical Supervision, Feedback, and Accountability; review Prompt Disclosure and Risk; log error category SUPERVISION-AND-ACCOUNTABILITY.',
	),
	array(
		'question_text'  => 'During a supervisory review, the parties must determine whether the plan and feedback process satisfy California’s accountability requirements. Which statement about required supervision by technology is most accurate under the current California BBS framework?',
		'option_a'       => 'Any telephone call counts as individual supervision if the case is discussed',
		'option_b'       => 'Videoconferencing is prohibited in private practice settings',
		'option_c'       => 'Live two-way videoconferencing may count when the supervisor determines and documents that it is appropriate and protects confidentiality; telephone supervision does not count',
		'option_d'       => 'A text-message exchange counts when it lasts at least one hour',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Telephone contact may be useful for support but does not satisfy the face-to-face component required for direct supervisor contact.
		B. Incorrect. Current law permits videoconference supervision in all settings when the requirements are met; the former setting restriction is not the current rule.
		C. Correct. The supervisor must determine and document appropriateness and preserve confidentiality. Telephone-only contact does not count toward licensure supervision.
		D. Incorrect. Text messages are not live face-to-face individual, triadic, or group supervision and do not satisfy the required format.
