	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A supervisor wants to review a video recording of a registered AMFT’s therapy session to evaluate clinical skills. What is required before using the recording for supervision?',
		'option_a'       => 'Only the registered AMFT’s verbal permission',
		'option_b'       => 'No consent because supervision automatically eliminates confidentiality',
		'option_c'       => 'Approval from another supervisee in the group',
		'option_d'       => 'The client’s written consent and appropriate protection of the recording',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. The registered AMFT cannot authorize disclosure of the client’s confidential session on the client’s behalf. The current supervisory framework specifies written client consent.
		B. Incorrect. Supervision does not eliminate confidentiality. Recordings create heightened privacy and security concerns and must be handled lawfully.
		C. Incorrect. Other supervisees have no authority to consent for the client and their approval is irrelevant.
		D. Correct. Direct observation or review of audio or video recordings may be used in an appropriate amount with the client’s written consent and secure handling.
