	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A supervisee continues to write vague progress notes after one general reminder. Which supervisory response best reflects accountability?',
		'option_a'       => 'Assume the supervisee understood and continue signing hours without review',
		'option_b'       => 'Identify specific deficiencies, review examples and standards, set measurable correction steps, and reassess the notes',
		'option_c'       => 'Criticize the supervisee’s attitude without discussing the records',
		'option_d'       => 'Delay all feedback until the relationship ends',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Accountability requires verifying whether feedback was understood and applied. Continuing to sign without review ignores the recurring concern.
		B. Correct. Specific evidence, clear standards, measurable expectations, and follow-up make feedback educational, fair, and protective.
		C. Incorrect. Character criticism is vague and does not teach the required documentation skill or establish what must change.
		D. Incorrect. End-of-relationship feedback is too late to protect current clients or support improvement.
		CTA Exam Strategy: The best remediation answer includes behavior, standard, action, timeline, and reassessment.
