	array(
		'question_text'  => 'Before accepting a third-party evaluation, a clinician must clarify who the client is, the purpose of the service, and how information may be used. Which statement best distinguishes a treating clinician from an evaluator?',
		'option_a'       => 'A treating clinician never makes assessments, while an evaluator always provides treatment',
		'option_b'       => 'An evaluator must always be employed by a court',
		'option_c'       => 'A treating clinician may ignore objectivity because the role is supportive',
		'option_d'       => 'Treatment primarily serves client care, while evaluation primarily answers a defined referral or decision question',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Treating clinicians assess routinely, and evaluators ordinarily do not provide treatment within the evaluation role.
		B. Incorrect. Evaluations may be requested by courts, employers, agencies, insurers, attorneys, or other authorized parties.
		C. Incorrect. Treating clinicians still need sound judgment and objectivity; support does not authorize bias or distortion.
		D. Correct. Purpose is the central distinction: treatment aims to help the client, while evaluation gathers and analyzes data to answer a defined question.
		CTA Exam Strategy: When two roles use similar clinical skills, compare their primary purpose and intended recipient.
