		'option_a'       => 'Preserve confidentiality, analyze privilege and the subpoena’s legal effect, distinguish fact testimony from expert opinion, and limit or decline opinions outside the treating role and competence',
		'option_b'       => 'Ignore the subpoena because treatment records are always privileged',
		'option_c'       => 'Provide every requested opinion because a subpoena creates expert status',
		'option_d'       => 'Call the opposing party and disclose the treatment record informally',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Correct. A subpoena requires legal analysis, not automatic disclosure or disregard, and it does not transform a treating clinician into a qualified expert on every issue.
		B. Incorrect. Privilege may apply, but a subpoena should not simply be ignored; appropriate objection, assertion, response, or legal guidance may be necessary.
		C. Incorrect. A subpoena compels attendance or production only as legally valid; it does not establish competence or expand the role.
		D. Incorrect. Informal disclosure to an opposing party would bypass authorization, privilege, and lawful procedure.
		CTA Exam Strategy: Combine confidentiality procedure with role limits. Avoid both “release everything” and “ignore all legal process.”
